On 2007-08-21, Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
>            SporkHack versus "Kitting Up"
>
> Am I the only one who thinks the spork emphasis on
> destroying the player's traditional RPG ability to
> "kit up" the PC and so stop worrying about some
> early critical kinds of problems while focusing on
> other, new issues the game presents, and thus
> extending the worry about having intrinsics
> game-long rather than confining it to the early and
> middle game, is just wrong-headed?

Huh? Are there any critical intrinsics in vanilla that cease to be critical
later in the game? News to me.

> What I see as needed to make the end game more
> interesting is more of the Lethe patch / Quest /
> Fort Ludius / Sokoban / Planes type of stuff, adding
> actual new interesting material to the game, rather
> than spork's approach of just making early forms of
> tedium last lots longer.
>
> What am I missing here?

There's two things that make the late game somewhat boring: The Gehennom
mazes (though there are shortcuts) and the fact that nothing really poses a
threat once you have the kit (well, you could just opt not to kit out
fully, ie, play a conduct game). Spork fixes the latter, for the former,
"fixes" like the heck^2 patch already exist.

> Huh? What's attractive about adding YAADs?

I think the point is that nethack should be a tactical game. Once you reach
the strategical point of possessing 100% reliable countermeasures to
everything, tactics in vanilla cease to be important. In an ideal game,
strategical preparation would give you some safety, but tactical errors
could still be fatal. SporkHack shouldn't add YAADs, but I don't think it
does. Just new kinds of YASD, which provide the "die and learn" experience
NetHack is so famous for.

Just my .02z
